panning from early March to late May 1982.\n\nHowever, I'm puzzled because none of these maturity dates align with the second or third week of June 1982. This suggests I might need to reconsider my interpretation of the bill categories or the specific timeframe.\n\nExamining the table more closely reveals an interesting pattern: the 26-week bills actually mature in June 1982. Specifically, I'll focus on the bills maturing during the second and third weeks of June: June 3rd and June 10th, which correspond to the weeks containing June 3-10 and June 10-17 respectively.\n\nFor 13-week bills, I calculate the issue date by subtracting 13 weeks from the maturity date. Applying this to June dates, I find two key bills: one issued on March 4, 1982, maturing June 3, 1982, and another issued on March 11, 1982, maturing June 10, 1982.\n\nHowever, the problem specifies \"last week of February 1982\" as the quotation period. This means I need to find bills quoted in February 1982 that will mature in early June.\n\nAnalyzing the 13-week bill cycle, I confirm that bills issued in late February would indeed mature in late May or early June. Specifically, bills from February 25 would mature around May 27-28, while bills from the last week of February would mature in the first week of June.\n\nFor the second week of June, a 13-week bill would need to be issued in the third week of March. Similarly, for the third week of June, the bill would need to be issued in the last week of March or first week of April.\n\nInterestingly, the current 13-week bills don't align perfectly with the June maturity dates. However, the 26-week bills from December 1981 and January 1982 do mature in June 1982, which could be a potential alternative perspective.\n\nBut the specific question is about 13-week bills. I need to reconsider how these bills are quoted and their precise maturity timelines.\n\nFrom the table, I can see two key 13-week bills with June 1982 maturity dates: one maturing on June 3 and another on June 10. These represent the second and third weeks of June 1982. Their corresponding issue dates would be March 4 and March 11 respectively, based on the 13-week cycle.\n\nThe critical point is that these bills were indeed quoted on February 26, 1982 - the last trading day of February - which aligns perfectly with the problem's timeframe.\n\nI'm now carefully examining the bank discount rates for these specific bills, noting they appear in the 26-week bill section of the table.\n\nThe discount rates are 12.53% for the 6/03/82 maturity and 12.57% for the 6/10/82 maturity. However, I'm uncertain whether these represent the 13-week or 26-week bill rates, given their occurrence in the 26-week bill entries.\n\nI'll need to cross-reference the original source document to clarify this potential discrepancy and confirm the precise bill categorization.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_07345a0f0adc46aba5a7b9bc","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"import json; data = json.load(open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1982_03.json')); print(json.dumps(data, indent=2))\""}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
